{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794540801723,"sessionID":"ses_e2d08c914ffeKxv7Qy4XSnG7U3","part":{"id":"prt_1d2f78ebb001LQIO4bkzNDuXuJ","sessionID":"ses_e2d08c914ffeKxv7Qy4XSnG7U3","messageID":"msg_1d2f78169001szcPRYewpeFWO8","type":"step-start","snapshot":"329012d74980dec2a681356adadfea5dc262ab07"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794540804109,"sessionID":"ses_e2d08c914ffeKxv7Qy4XSnG7U3","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2f796c6001Cze1ciSbWqvi2X","sessionID":"ses_e2d08c914ffeKxv7Qy4XSnG7U3","messageID":"msg_1d2f78169001szcPRYewpeFWO8","type":"tool","id":"call_9570f09883bc4fb3a41f01db","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md, lines 1-245\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication\"\n3: description: \"Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"cut filler\"\n6:   - \"empty opener\"\n7:   - \"match length to the question\"\n8:   - \"no tables\"\n9:   - \"can you say that more plainly\"\n10:   - \"I don't follow\"\n11:   - \"too abstract\"\n12:   - \"number the steps\"\n13:   - \"item cap\"\n14:   - \"reply too long\"\n15:   - \"say each fact once\"\n16:   - \"one idea per sentence\"\n17:   - \"plain words\"\n18:   - \"em dash\"\n19:   - \"reads like terminal output\"\n20:   - \"concise is not compressed\"\n21:   - \"recommend one option\"\n22:   - \"buried the verdict\"\n23:   - \"say where you are going\"\n24:   - \"time estimate\"\n25: importance_tier: important\n26: contextType: reference\n27: version: 1.5.0.0\n28: ---\n29: \n30: # Rule: Communication\n31: \n32: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before writing any substantive reply.\n33: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n34: \n35: ## Fires when\n36: \n37: - About to write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out, a status.\n38: - Drafting or editing a sentence a reader will read.\n39: - About to present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off.\n40: - About to start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside.\n41: - About to report what a long autonomous run found.\n42: - The reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Write so the reader can act after one pass: the answer first, and nothing in the reply\n47: that does not carry information.**\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## 1. THE REGISTER YOU ARE IN\n52: \n53: **While working: clipped.** Act rather than narrate. Open with the result, not with\n54: \"I'll now\" or \"Let me\". Batch the tool calls and report at checkpoints.\n55: \n56: **At a boundary: dense.** You are at a boundary whenever the reader is about to decide\n57: something, act on something or take the work over: a handoff, a close-out, a decision\n58: point. Verdict first, then the receipts. Reason about the problem, not about yourself.\n59: Everything else is working.\n60: \n61: **Complex topic, simple words.** When the subject is technical or layered, explain it plainly the first time, not only after the reader asks: what it is, why it matters to them, what they do next. Use a term only when the reader needs it, and define it on first use. Simpler words, same claim: never drop a caveat or a number to get there.\n62: \n63: The failure this prevents: the wrong register. Narration nobody reads, the verdict buried\n64: in working notes or the working notes inflated into a report.\n65: \n66: ---\n67: \n68: ## 2. LENGTH\n69: \n70: **Match length to the question.** Length is earned by the reader's need, never by the work you did to get there.\n71: \n72: **Choose by reader impact before you write.** Decide who will read this and what they already hold. Keep what the reader would notice, act on or\n73: decide differently because of. Merge the items that have the same effect for them. How the\n74: work was done stays out unless it changes how far the reader can trust the result.\n75: \n76: **Concise is not compressed.** Concise cuts what the reader does not need and stops. Compressed cuts further and takes the connective tissue with it: the because, the second half of a contrast, the noun that a later \"this\" points back to. After a cut, check the joints. Every cut this file asks for answers to that floor.\n77: \n78: The failure this prevents: the answer is in there, and they did not find it, under a\n79: record of the work they never needed. Or the cut went too far, and the reader rebuilds the\n80: argument the reply no longer states.\n81: \n82: **No tables in a reply,** except the in-flight block in\n83: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §6. Use a sentence for one or two\n84: facts and bullets for parallel items.\n85: \n86: The failure this prevents: the reader parses a grid to learn what one sentence would\n87: have said.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 3. CUT FILLER\n92: \n93: Every sentence carries information. The recurring offenders:\n94: \n95: - **Empty openers:** \"Great question\", \"Let me take a look\", \"I'll now\".\n96: - **Restated summaries:** repeating back what you just said, one abstraction level up.\n97: - **Vague warnings:** \"be careful with this\", \"this can be tricky\", naming no failure. A caveat earns its place by naming a failure that can happen here.\n98: - **Corporate and marketing register:** \"robust\", \"seamless\", \"leverage\", \"best-in-class\".\n99: - **Narrating the obvious:** announcing a tool call the reader can see the result of.\n100: - **Leaked scaffolding:** a runtime line that tells you to plan privately, list what you\n101:   need next or batch your calls is answered in reasoning, never in the reply.\n102: \n103: **Say each fact once.** Each part of the reply has one job: the first lines carry the\n104: outcome, the body carries the detail, and the end carries the receipts and the handback. A\n105: sentence that repeats an earlier one goes, or it says something the reader does not know\n106: yet.\n107: \n108: The failure this prevents: filler trains the reader to skim, and then they skim the\n109: sentence that mattered.\n110: \n111: ---\n112: \n113: ## 4. THE ANSWER FIRST\n114: \n115: **The first line carries the payload.** The answer, the verdict or the action, in the\n116: first sentence, not a label for it. Test: read the first line on its own. If it told you the outcome, it did its job. Four habits break it: announcing (\"I will now\n117: check the tests\"), labelling (\"Overview:\"), the fragment opener (\"Context.\") and the set-up\n118: that promises the good part in a moment.\n119: \n120: **The rest of the outcome follows at once.** What the work concluded, what changed and what\n121: to do next are in the reader's hands by the second line. The receipts come after that, and\n122: the handback comes last, per [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n123: End when the answer is done. The closing-deletion test: a last line that only asks whether\n124: anything else is needed, or recaps what the reply just said, deletes.\n125: \n126: **Reach the verdict by analysis, then put it first.** The order of the reply is not the order of the thinking. If you cannot state the verdict yet, say that. A named uncertainty is a verdict about the state of the evidence.\n127: \n128: **Recommend one path.** Name its main trade-off, and mention an alternative only when it could change the decision. Mark must-do work apart from nice-to-have. State an assumption you made where evidence was missing, because the reader can correct a visible assumption and cannot correct a silent one.\n129: \n130: **An ambiguous request still gets the answer first.** When two readings lead to different answers, answer the likelier one and say in one line which reading you took. When they lead to materially different work, ask before starting it, per [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md) §1. When you deliver a different version of the ask, the first lines say what changed and why.\n131: \n132: The failure this prevents: a payload that waits behind an announcement or a label, a survey\n133: of options where one recommendation was owed, a swapped or misread request nobody catches,\n134: or a last line that is a farewell and adds nothing.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 5. HOW THE REPLY MOVES\n139: \n140: **Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward.** A reader who lands mid-reply can act on the paragraph in front of them, and each one picks up where the last one landed: what changed, what it implies, what comes next.\n141: \n142: **Number the steps when there is more than one.** Anything the reader must run, check or\n143: answer gets its own number, one step per line, the number at the start of the line. Numbers\n144: bracketed inside a sentence, (1) then (2) then (3), are a paragraph wearing numbers.\n145: \n146: **No group shows more than five items.** Split a longer set into labelled groups of five or\n147: fewer, or show the five that matter most and say in one line how many are held back, \"the\n148: first five of nine\". The rest is retained, not discarded, and appears when the reader asks.\n149: \n150: **Suppress the tangent.** The reply answers the question it was asked. A second issue worth\n151: raising gets offered once, at the end, in one line. The reader decides whether it happens.\n152: \n153: The failure this prevents: the reader assembles the order themselves, loses track of which\n154: step they have done, mistakes a shortened list for the complete one, or waits for the\n155: answer behind a second issue.\n156: \n157: ---\n158: \n159: ## 6. SENTENCES AND WORDS\n160: \n161: **One idea per sentence.** Short and declarative. When a sentence stacks clauses, split it. Vary the rhythm, and prefer prose when a list would fragment a single argument.\n162: \n163: **State the relation between sentences.** A sentence says how it attaches to the one before: because, but, therefore, for example. When it explains how something works, it names the moving parts: what acts, what it acts on, what changes.\n164: \n165: **Plain words by default.** Reserve exact names for the things that have them: languages, frameworks, APIs, dependencies, commands. Introduce unavoidable jargon one term at a time, in a sentence that defines it by use.\n166: \n167: **No terse machine register.** Replace arrows and symbols standing in for verbs with action words. For stacked noun phrases, say who does what, using the [HVR nominalization guidance](../skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md#nominalization-and-stacked-compression). Explain each ID, abbreviation or status code in plain words, and say what a bare count or label measures. Turn fragments into complete sentences.\n168: \n169: **Name an identifier only where the reader acts on it.** A path, hash, flag or ID earns its place when the reader will run, open or check it. Elsewhere, say what the thing is. Keep to one or two numbers a sentence, and when the numbers are the point, give each its own sentence or bullet.\n170: \n171: The failure this prevents: a technically correct reply the reader has to decode, where\n172: they supply a link the writer never stated and cannot find the few names and figures they\n173: need.\n174: \n175: ---\n176: \n177: ## 7. PUNCTUATION THE READER TRIPS ON\n178: \n179: - **Never use an em dash.** Use a comma, a full stop or a colon, whichever the sentence wanted.\n180: - **No semicolon.** Two sentences, or a conjunction.\n181: - **No serial comma.** Drop it before the `and` or `or` that closes a list.\n182: \n183: The failure this prevents: dashes read as authored voice to a human and as a tell to a\n184: reader who has seen a lot of generated text.\n185: \n186: The full standard is the Human Voice Rules, which `sk-doc` routes to. Load all of it when writing a document. A reply takes its voice half, the directives, vocabulary lists and tell lists, and leaves its document-structure half.\n187: \n188: ---\n189: \n190: ## 8. LONG STRETCHES AND WHAT THEY FOUND\n191: \n192: **Say where you are going before a long stretch.** Before work the reader cannot see inside, post the intended path: a short numbered list of what you will do and what to expect at each checkpoint. Update it when it changes. Clipped means not narrating each step. It never means starting without saying where you are going.\n193: \n194: **Attach a concrete time estimate when the stretch is long.** Minutes or hours, based on a named earlier run, or marked as an assumption. An estimate informs and never gates. Outside that case, do not open a task with one nobody asked for, and never use one to argue against starting. When the task is too big for one pass, name the smallest slice worth delivering, start there and say what is deferred.\n195: \n196: **A synthesis is not a file path.** When a long run produces findings, the message carries the answer, the few findings behind it, the uncertainty that matters and what was ruled out. The artifact path follows them. Per-iteration narration and internal metrics stay out. Where a run has its own completion contract, as the deep-loop modes do, that contract wins.\n197: \n198: The failure this prevents: twenty minutes of silence, then a result the reader has to\n199: reverse-engineer a plan from, or a path and a count handed over in place of a report.\n200: \n201: ---\n202: \n203: ## 9. WHEN THE READER DID NOT FOLLOW\n204: \n205: \"I don't follow\", \"what?\" or \"too abstract\" calls for a change of modality: a concrete\n206: example, a numbered sequence, a smaller first step or a picture using the runtime's visual\n207: capability. Do not repeat the same explanation at greater length.\n208: \n209: \"say that more plainly\", \"in simple terms\" or \"rewrite that\" calls for a plain\n210: re-render: a copy edit that rewords without reordering, cutting or adding. Every claim,\n211: number, caveat and conclusion keeps its strength. Code, commands, paths, identifiers,\n212: quotations and numbers stay byte-exact. The wording standard is\n213: [hvr-rules.md](../skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md). If a\n214: plainer word would change what a sentence claims, keep the original.\n215: \n216: The failure this prevents: the same explanation repeats, or the plain rewrite changes the claim.\n217: \n218: ---\n219: \n220: ## 10. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n221: \n222: - **Not a constraint on rigor.** Nothing here softens a claim, a caveat, or a verification standard owned by [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) or [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n223: - **Not a voice to perform.** When honoring a rule here would weaken the answer, keep the answer.\n224: - **Not a license to omit.** \"Match length to the question\" is about the reader's need,\n225:   never about leaving out what they have to know.\n226: - **Not licence to skip the analysis.** Verdict first is an ordering of the writing, never\n227:   a shortcut in the reasoning.\n228: \n229: ---\n230: \n231: ## 11. SELF-CHECK\n232: \n233: - [ ] The first line carries the answer or the action, not a label, an announcement or a setup, and the outcome is complete by the second line.\n234: - [ ] I reached the verdict by analysis, recommended one path with its trade-off and stated the assumptions I made.\n235: - [ ] Where the request was ambiguous or I delivered a different version of it, the first lines say which reading I took and why.\n236: - [ ] Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward, and multi-step work reads as a numbered list.\n237: - [ ] No group runs past five items, nothing was dropped to keep it under, and a tangent appears once at the end or not at all.\n238: - [ ] Every sentence carries information, each fact appears once, and no runtime instruction to plan privately was copied into the reply.\n239: - [ ] Length matches what the reader asked, no table stands in a reply, and nothing was compressed past the point where the reader has to re-expand it.\n240: - [ ] Every sentence carries one idea in plain words and says how it attaches to the one before.\n241: - [ ] Arrows, bare IDs, counts and fragments were turned into sentences, and every path, hash or flag is one the reader will act on.\n242: - [ ] No em dash, no semicolon, no serial comma.\n243: - [ ] Before a long stretch I said where I was going, with a concrete time estimate when it was long, and no estimate argued against starting.\n244: - [ ] A synthesis I reported carries its findings, not just its path and its counts.\n245: - [ ] When the reader did not follow, I changed modality, and a plain re-render kept every claim, number and caveat at the same strength.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md, lines 1-245\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication\"\n3: description: \"Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"cut filler\"\n6:   - \"empty opener\"\n7:   - \"match length to the question\"\n8:   - \"no tables\"\n9:   - \"can you say that more plainly\"\n10:   - \"I don't follow\"\n11:   - \"too abstract\"\n12:   - \"number the steps\"\n13:   - \"item cap\"\n14:   - \"reply too long\"\n15:   - \"say each fact once\"\n16:   - \"one idea per sentence\"\n17:   - \"plain words\"\n18:   - \"em dash\"\n19:   - \"reads like terminal output\"\n20:   - \"concise is not compressed\"\n21:   - \"recommend one option\"\n22:   - \"buried the verdict\"\n23:   - \"say where you are going\"\n24:   - \"time estimate\"\n25: importance_tier: important\n26: contextType: reference\n27: version: 1.5.0.0\n28: ---\n29: \n30: # Rule: Communication\n31: \n32: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before writing any substantive reply.\n33: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n34: \n35: ## Fires when\n36: \n37: - About to write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out, a status.\n38: - Drafting or editing a sentence a reader will read.\n39: - About to present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off.\n40: - About to start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside.\n41: - About to report what a long autonomous run found.\n42: - The reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Write so the reader can act after one pass: the answer first, and nothing in the reply\n47: that does not carry information.**\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## 1. THE REGISTER YOU ARE IN\n52: \n53: **While working: clipped.** Act rather than narrate. Open with the result, not with\n54: \"I'll now\" or \"Let me\". Batch the tool calls and report at checkpoints.\n55: \n56: **At a boundary: dense.** You are at a boundary whenever the reader is about to decide\n57: something, act on something or take the work over: a handoff, a close-out, a decision\n58: point. Verdict first, then the receipts. Reason about the problem, not about yourself.\n59: Everything else is working.\n60: \n61: **Complex topic, simple words.** When the subject is technical or layered, explain it plainly the first time, not only after the reader asks: what it is, why it matters to them, what they do next. Use a term only when the reader needs it, and define it on first use. Simpler words, same claim: never drop a caveat or a number to get there.\n62: \n63: The failure this prevents: the wrong register. Narration nobody reads, the verdict buried\n64: in working notes or the working notes inflated into a report.\n65: \n66: ---\n67: \n68: ## 2. LENGTH\n69: \n70: **Match length to the question.** Length is earned by the reader's need, never by the work you did to get there.\n71: \n72: **Choose by reader impact before you write.** Decide who will read this and what they already hold. Keep what the reader would notice, act on or\n73: decide differently because of. Merge the items that have the same effect for them. How the\n74: work was done stays out unless it changes how far the reader can trust the result.\n75: \n76: **Concise is not compressed.** Concise cuts what the reader does not need and stops. Compressed cuts further and takes the connective tissue with it: the because, the second half of a contrast, the noun that a later \"this\" points back to. After a cut, check the joints. Every cut this file asks for answers to that floor.\n77: \n78: The failure this prevents: the answer is in there, and they did not find it, under a\n79: record of the work they never needed. Or the cut went too far, and the reader rebuilds the\n80: argument the reply no longer states.\n81: \n82: **No tables in a reply,** except the in-flight block in\n83: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §6. Use a sentence for one or two\n84: facts and bullets for parallel items.\n85: \n86: The failure this prevents: the reader parses a grid to learn what one sentence would\n87: have said.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 3. CUT FILLER\n92: \n93: Every sentence carries information. The recurring offenders:\n94: \n95: - **Empty openers:** \"Great question\", \"Let me take a look\", \"I'll now\".\n96: - **Restated summaries:** repeating back what you just said, one abstraction level up.\n97: - **Vague warnings:** \"be careful with this\", \"this can be tricky\", naming no failure. A caveat earns its place by naming a failure that can happen here.\n98: - **Corporate and marketing register:** \"robust\", \"seamless\", \"leverage\", \"best-in-class\".\n99: - **Narrating the obvious:** announcing a tool call the reader can see the result of.\n100: - **Leaked scaffolding:** a runtime line that tells you to plan privately, list what you\n101:   need next or batch your calls is answered in reasoning, never in the reply.\n102: \n103: **Say each fact once.** Each part of the reply has one job: the first lines carry the\n104: outcome, the body carries the detail, and the end carries the receipts and the handback. A\n105: sentence that repeats an earlier one goes, or it says something the reader does not know\n106: yet.\n107: \n108: The failure this prevents: filler trains the reader to skim, and then they skim the\n109: sentence that mattered.\n110: \n111: ---\n112: \n113: ## 4. THE ANSWER FIRST\n114: \n115: **The first line carries the payload.** The answer, the verdict or the action, in the\n116: first sentence, not a label for it. Test: read the first line on its own. If it told you the outcome, it did its job. Four habits break it: announcing (\"I will now\n117: check the tests\"), labelling (\"Overview:\"), the fragment opener (\"Context.\") and the set-up\n118: that promises the good part in a moment.\n119: \n120: **The rest of the outcome follows at once.** What the work concluded, what changed and what\n121: to do next are in the reader's hands by the second line. The receipts come after that, and\n122: the handback comes last, per [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n123: End when the answer is done. The closing-deletion test: a last line that only asks whether\n124: anything else is needed, or recaps what the reply just said, deletes.\n125: \n126: **Reach the verdict by analysis, then put it first.** The order of the reply is not the order of the thinking. If you cannot state the verdict yet, say that. A named uncertainty is a verdict about the state of the evidence.\n127: \n128: **Recommend one path.** Name its main trade-off, and mention an alternative only when it could change the decision. Mark must-do work apart from nice-to-have. State an assumption you made where evidence was missing, because the reader can correct a visible assumption and cannot correct a silent one.\n129: \n130: **An ambiguous request still gets the answer first.** When two readings lead to different answers, answer the likelier one and say in one line which reading you took. When they lead to materially different work, ask before starting it, per [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md) §1. When you deliver a different version of the ask, the first lines say what changed and why.\n131: \n132: The failure this prevents: a payload that waits behind an announcement or a label, a survey\n133: of options where one recommendation was owed, a swapped or misread request nobody catches,\n134: or a last line that is a farewell and adds nothing.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 5. HOW THE REPLY MOVES\n139: \n140: **Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward.** A reader who lands mid-reply can act on the paragraph in front of them, and each one picks up where the last one landed: what changed, what it implies, what comes next.\n141: \n142: **Number the steps when there is more than one.** Anything the reader must run, check or\n143: answer gets its own number, one step per line, the number at the start of the line. Numbers\n144: bracketed inside a sentence, (1) then (2) then (3), are a paragraph wearing numbers.\n145: \n146: **No group shows more than five items.** Split a longer set into labelled groups of five or\n147: fewer, or show the five that matter most and say in one line how many are held back, \"the\n148: first five of nine\". The rest is retained, not discarded, and appears when the reader asks.\n149: \n150: **Suppress the tangent.** The reply answers the question it was asked. A second issue worth\n151: raising gets offered once, at the end, in one line. The reader decides whether it happens.\n152: \n153: The failure this prevents: the reader assembles the order themselves, loses track of which\n154: step they have done, mistakes a shortened list for the complete one, or waits for the\n155: answer behind a second issue.\n156: \n157: ---\n158: \n159: ## 6. SENTENCES AND WORDS\n160: \n161: **One idea per sentence.** Short and declarative. When a sentence stacks clauses, split it. Vary the rhythm, and prefer prose when a list would fragment a single argument.\n162: \n163: **State the relation between sentences.** A sentence says how it attaches to the one before: because, but, therefore, for example. When it explains how something works, it names the moving parts: what acts, what it acts on, what changes.\n164: \n165: **Plain words by default.** Reserve exact names for the things that have them: languages, frameworks, APIs, dependencies, commands. Introduce unavoidable jargon one term at a time, in a sentence that defines it by use.\n166: \n167: **No terse machine register.** Replace arrows and symbols standing in for verbs with action words. For stacked noun phrases, say who does what, using the [HVR nominalization guidance](../skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md#nominalization-and-stacked-compression). Explain each ID, abbreviation or status code in plain words, and say what a bare count or label measures. Turn fragments into complete sentences.\n168: \n169: **Name an identifier only where the reader acts on it.** A path, hash, flag or ID earns its place when the reader will run, open or check it. Elsewhere, say what the thing is. Keep to one or two numbers a sentence, and when the numbers are the point, give each its own sentence or bullet.\n170: \n171: The failure this prevents: a technically correct reply the reader has to decode, where\n172: they supply a link the writer never stated and cannot find the few names and figures they\n173: need.\n174: \n175: ---\n176: \n177: ## 7. PUNCTUATION THE READER TRIPS ON\n178: \n179: - **Never use an em dash.** Use a comma, a full stop or a colon, whichever the sentence wanted.\n180: - **No semicolon.** Two sentences, or a conjunction.\n181: - **No serial comma.** Drop it before the `and` or `or` that closes a list.\n182: \n183: The failure this prevents: dashes read as authored voice to a human and as a tell to a\n184: reader who has seen a lot of generated text.\n185: \n186: The full standard is the Human Voice Rules, which `sk-doc` routes to. Load all of it when writing a document. A reply takes its voice half, the directives, vocabulary lists and tell lists, and leaves its document-structure half.\n187: \n188: ---\n189: \n190: ## 8. LONG STRETCHES AND WHAT THEY FOUND\n191: \n192: **Say where you are going before a long stretch.** Before work the reader cannot see inside, post the intended path: a short numbered list of what you will do and what to expect at each checkpoint. Update it when it changes. Clipped means not narrating each step. It never means starting without saying where you are going.\n193: \n194: **Attach a concrete time estimate when the stretch is long.** Minutes or hours, based on a named earlier run, or marked as an assumption. An estimate informs and never gates. Outside that case, do not open a task with one nobody asked for, and never use one to argue against starting. When the task is too big for one pass, name the smallest slice worth delivering, start there and say what is deferred.\n195: \n196: **A synthesis is not a file path.** When a long run produces findings, the message carries the answer, the few findings behind it, the uncertainty that matters and what was ruled out. The artifact path follows them. Per-iteration narration and internal metrics stay out. Where a run has its own completion contract, as the deep-loop modes do, that contract wins.\n197: \n198: The failure this prevents: twenty minutes of silence, then a result the reader has to\n199: reverse-engineer a plan from, or a path and a count handed over in place of a report.\n200: \n201: ---\n202: \n203: ## 9. WHEN THE READER DID NOT FOLLOW\n204: \n205: \"I don't follow\", \"what?\" or \"too abstract\" calls for a change of modality: a concrete\n206: example, a numbered sequence, a smaller first step or a picture using the runtime's visual\n207: capability. Do not repeat the same explanation at greater length.\n208: \n209: \"say that more plainly\", \"in simple terms\" or \"rewrite that\" calls for a plain\n210: re-render: a copy edit that rewords without reordering, cutting or adding. Every claim,\n211: number, caveat and conclusion keeps its strength. Code, commands, paths, identifiers,\n212: quotations and numbers stay byte-exact. The wording standard is\n213: [hvr-rules.md](../skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md). If a\n214: plainer word would change what a sentence claims, keep the original.\n215: \n216: The failure this prevents: the same explanation repeats, or the plain rewrite changes the claim.\n217: \n218: ---\n219: \n220: ## 10. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n221: \n222: - **Not a constraint on rigor.** Nothing here softens a claim, a caveat, or a verification standard owned by [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) or [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n223: - **Not a voice to perform.** When honoring a rule here would weaken the answer, keep the answer.\n224: - **Not a license to omit.** \"Match length to the question\" is about the reader's need,\n225:   never about leaving out what they have to know.\n226: - **Not licence to skip the analysis.** Verdict first is an ordering of the writing, never\n227:   a shortcut in the reasoning.\n228: \n229: ---\n230: \n231: ## 11. SELF-CHECK\n232: \n233: - [ ] The first line carries the answer or the action, not a label, an announcement or a setup, and the outcome is complete by the second line.\n234: - [ ] I reached the verdict by analysis, recommended one path with its trade-off and stated the assumptions I made.\n235: - [ ] Where the request was ambiguous or I delivered a different version of it, the first lines say which reading I took and why.\n236: - [ ] Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward, and multi-step work reads as a numbered list.\n237: - [ ] No group runs past five items, nothing was dropped to keep it under, and a tangent appears once at the end or not at all.\n238: - [ ] Every sentence carries information, each fact appears once, and no runtime instruction to plan privately was copied into the reply.\n239: - [ ] Length matches what the reader asked, no table stands in a reply, and nothing was compressed past the point where the reader has to re-expand it.\n240: - [ ] Every sentence carries one idea in plain words and says how it attaches to the one before.\n241: - [ ] Arrows, bare IDs, counts and fragments were turned into sentences, and every path, hash or flag is one the reader will act on.\n242: - [ ] No em dash, no semicolon, no serial comma.\n243: - [ ] Before a long stretch I said where I was going, with a concrete time estimate when it was long, and no estimate argued against starting.\n244: - [ ] A synthesis I reported carries its findings, not just its path and its counts.\n245: - [ ] When the reader did not follow, I changed modality, and a plain re-render kept every claim, number and caveat at the same strength."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794540803782,"end":1794540804109}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794540804134,"sessionID":"ses_e2d08c914ffeKxv7Qy4XSnG7U3","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2f79532001wBMy4C5U94jtlY","sessionID":"ses_e2d08c914ffeKxv7Qy4XSnG7U3","messageID":"msg_1d2f78169001szcPRYewpeFWO8","type":"tool","id":"call_50b67852054f445bbddf4d12","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md, lines 1-132\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Command bridges\"\n3: description: \"Authored command-bridge inputs, compatibility evidence and the generated projection consumed by advisor scoring surfaces.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"command bridges\"\n6:   - \"generated command bridge projection\"\n7:   - \"command bridge compatibility\"\n8: ---\n9: \n10: # Command bridges\n11: \n12: ---\n13: \n14: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n15: \n16: `command-bridges/` is the source boundary for command metadata that is not yet represented uniformly by every scoring surface. The deriver combines live skill metadata with the authored compatibility inputs and writes the generated JSON projection.\n17: \n18: The doctor commands are a third source: the deriver reads `.skilled/commands/doctor/_routes.yaml` and writes one bridge per doctor route and per standalone doctor command and per MCP sub-route.\n19: \n20: Each hub's `mode-registry.json` is a fourth source: the deriver reads the `command` of every mode and writes the `MODE_COMMANDS` table the advisor uses to attach a slash command to a hub recommendation.\n21: \n22: ---\n23: \n24: ## 2. CONTENTS\n25: \n26: | File | Responsibility | Authority |\n27: |---|---|---|\n28: | `command-bridges.generated.json` | Stores the derived command-bridge projection. | Generated, do not edit |\n29: | `derive-command-bridges.cjs` | Reads live metadata and authored inputs, validates uniqueness and writes the projection. | Authored deriver |\n30: | `scoring-compatibility.json` | Holds TypeScript and Python scoring compatibility mappings. | Authored input |\n31: | `shadow-diff.md` | Records comparison evidence between generated and live bridge authorities. | Authored evidence |\n32: \n33: ---\n34: \n35: ## 3. DERIVATION FLOW\n36: \n37: ```text\n38: skill command metadata files\n39: scoring-compatibility.json\n40: .skilled/commands/doctor/_routes.yaml\n41: hub mode-registry.json files\n42:                          |\n43:                          v\n44:               derive-command-bridges.cjs\n45:                          |\n46:                          v\n47:               command-bridges.generated.json\n48: ```\n49: \n50: The deriver also updates generated projection blocks in the live TypeScript and Python scoring sources. Its `GENERATED COMMAND ROWS` block in `lib/command-attach.ts` holds `COMMAND_ROWS`, one row per id a scorer can return for an enabled entry, covering the entry id and every enabled runtime id. Each row carries the runnable slash command, its derived policy and the owning skill. The same block holds `COMMAND_POLICIES`, one policy per runnable string, so a surface that attaches a command can name its policy without a second lookup. It writes the `MODE_COMMANDS` block into the same file, one row per hub mode that names a command, and fails when a mode names a command with no file under `.skilled/commands/`. Each TypeScript bridge in `lib/scorer/projection.ts` carries an `ownerSkill`, the skill that owns its command. The generated JSON and generated source blocks are outputs, not hand-edited inputs.\n51: \n52: Each bridge row with TypeScript enabled also carries an `embeddingText`, the plain sentence a scoring vector is built from. A Python-only row carries none. A metadata row leads with its `userIntent.job` sentence and adds every `userIntent.ownedSignals` phrase, because both are written in the asker's voice. The entry `description` stands in for the job only when the record states no job. A doctor row builds it from the router frontmatter `description` and the `trigger_phrases` of the route. The deriver drops each slash command string before the parts are joined, because a name left in would hand the scorer a second copy of the token it already matches literally. The field is derived on every run and never authored, so edit the sources rather than the JSON.\n53: \n54: ---\n55: \n56: ## 4. DOCTOR BRIDGES\n57: \n58: Ids are `command-doctor-<command>-<target>` when the doctor command owns more than one route, and `command-doctor-<command>` otherwise. `skillId` and `ownerMode` come from the router's `skill_agent` tag. A router without the tag fails the deriver with a named error.\n59: \n60: The runnable string is the command plus its target when the command owns more than one route. A standalone entry may set `runnable`, as `/doctor:env` does with `/doctor:env list`.\n61: \n62: A new route needs a hand-written entry in both the `typescript` and `python` lists in `scoring-compatibility.json`. Each entry carries only `inventoryId`, `order`, `id` (equal to `inventoryId`) and an empty `description`.\n63: \n64: The deriver fills the TypeScript name, keywords, intent signals and domains from the manifest, and the Python slash marker from the runnable string. A route marked `default_target` in `_routes.yaml` also gets the bare command (for example `/doctor:ci`) as a TypeScript keyword and a Python slash marker, so the bare typed command reaches it. A typed `<command> <target>` names that target, so the bare command inside it does not also match the default target. Python matches a doctor bridge only on a typed slash command.\n65: \n66: A doctor bridge passes on its own wording (an explicit-author phrase match). When none passes and no `/doctor:` command was typed, the scorer asks `/doctor:helper`'s own ranker, `doctor-helper-match.cjs`, whether the prompt describes a doctor problem. If it does, `command-doctor-helper` passes, and `/doctor:helper` then names the command. With no skill passing, a ranker score of 10 or more is enough, or 6 or more when the prompt names a problem. Beside a passing skill, the prompt must name a wrong state or a health check, such as `is not registered`, `never converges` or `turn off`. A `/doctor:` command counts as typed when the prompt starts with `/doctor:` or when it directly follows `run`, `execute`, `start`, `launch`, `use` or `kick off` outside quotes and backticks. A quoted command or a bare mid-sentence mention does not count. A typed command puts its own passing bridge first. A prompt that contains one of a bridge's `lead_phrases` also puts that passing bridge first, so `check my environment variables` leads with `/doctor:env`. For other prompts, a passing skill keeps first place and passing doctor bridges sit right after the second passing skill or the only passing skill. This keeps a doctor line within the CLI's three shown results. A typed command shows its matching runnable string on the top line. A skill-first brief shows `Also: /doctor:<command> <target>` on the second line. The Python fallback matches a doctor bridge only on a typed slash command.\n67: \n68: Each `mcp_subroutes` entry becomes the bridge `command-doctor-mcp-<sub_action>`, with the runnable string `/doctor:mcp <sub_action>` and its owner from the `skill_agent` tag in `mcp.md`. Every derived doctor bridge is active in both scorers, and `doctor-phrase-reach.cjs` proves each one.\n69: \n70: Check every admitted phrase with `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/doctor-phrase-reach.cjs`.\n71: \n72: Every command bridge, not only a doctor bridge, passes on its own wording. `requireCommandPhraseAnchor` in `lib/scorer/fusion.ts` drops a passing command row below threshold unless the explicit-author lane matched one of its phrases or its slash string. The one other way in is meaning: a real embedder cosine of at least 0.72 against the row's `embeddingText` vector, with a 0.02 lead over the next command row. That needs the local embedder, so without it every command row passes on its wording alone. A prompt that asks for no change rules out every command on meaning, and a read-only question admits only a bridge marked `readOnly`.\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: ## 5. STANDALONE SKILL COMMANDS\n77: \n78: A standalone skill has a `command-metadata.json` and no `mode-registry.json`, as `sk-prompt` and `system-spec-kit` do. Its commands are active in both scorers by construction and need no row in `scoring-compatibility.json`. The deriver builds each row from the metadata entry:\n79: \n80: - TypeScript: the `userIntent.ownedSignals` phrases become the keywords and intent signals, the command's namespace and `command` become the domains, and a `read-only` `mutating` class sets `readOnly: true`.\n81: - Python: the slash markers are the command with and without its leading slash, and `owningSkill` is the skill.\n82: \n83: A command needs at least two `userIntent.ownedSignals`, or the deriver fails. A hand row in `scoring-compatibility.json` still wins where one exists. Generated rows take orders after the highest hand-written order.\n84: \n85: ---\n86: \n87: ## 6. HUMAN EDIT BOUNDARY\n88: \n89: Humans may edit `derive-command-bridges.cjs`, `scoring-compatibility.json` and `shadow-diff.md`. Regenerate `command-bridges.generated.json` after changing an authored input or the deriver. Do not edit the generated JSON directly.\n90: \n91: ---\n92: \n93: ## 7. VALIDATION\n94: \n95: Run the deriver in check mode from the repository root:\n96: \n97: ```bash\n98: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/derive-command-bridges.cjs --check\n99: ```\n100: \n101: ---\n102: \n103: ## 8. RISK KEYS AND COMMAND POLICY\n104: \n105: The command tree `.skilled/commands/<namespace>/<name>.md` owns which commands exist. The deriver fails when a command file has no `command-metadata.json` entry and no doctor route. It fails again when a declaration names a command with no file.\n106: \n107: A `command-metadata.json` entry and a doctor route or action carry the same risk keys. A value outside the accepted set fails the deriver before any policy is derived.\n108: \n109: | Risk key | Accepted values | Applies to |\n110: |---|---|---|\n111: | `mutating` | `read-only`, `add-only`, `mutates` | Every metadata entry and every doctor route or action |\n112: | `gate3_location` | A non-empty string naming what the command writes | Required on every metadata entry |\n113: | `ask_first` | `settings`, `outside-repo`, `spend`, `relaunch` | Optional on both records |\n114: | `write_scope` | `spec-folder` | Optional on both records |\n115: \n116: `derivePolicy` reads those keys in order and stops at the first match.\n117: \n118: | Step | Condition | Policy |\n119: |---|---|---|\n120: | 1 | `ask_first` lists at least one reason | `ask` |\n121: | 2 | A doctor route or action classed `mutates` | `ask` |\n122: | 3 | `mutating` is `read-only` | `run` |\n123: | 4 | `mutating` is `add-only` with `write_scope` set to `spec-folder` | `run` |\n124: | 5 | Any other record | `start` |\n125: \n126: `commandPolicies` in `command-bridges.generated.json` holds one record per command, or one record per route or action for a doctor command. Each record carries five keys: `command`, `route`, `mutating`, `askFirst`, `policy`. Nothing scores on it yet.\n127: \n128: ---\n129: \n130: ## 9. RELATED\n131: \n132: - [`Skill-advisor scripts`](../README.md)","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md, lines 1-132\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Command bridges\"\n3: description: \"Authored command-bridge inputs, compatibility evidence and the generated projection consumed by advisor scoring surfaces.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"command bridges\"\n6:   - \"generated command bridge projection\"\n7:   - \"command bridge compatibility\"\n8: ---\n9: \n10: # Command bridges\n11: \n12: ---\n13: \n14: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n15: \n16: `command-bridges/` is the source boundary for command metadata that is not yet represented uniformly by every scoring surface. The deriver combines live skill metadata with the authored compatibility inputs and writes the generated JSON projection.\n17: \n18: The doctor commands are a third source: the deriver reads `.skilled/commands/doctor/_routes.yaml` and writes one bridge per doctor route and per standalone doctor command and per MCP sub-route.\n19: \n20: Each hub's `mode-registry.json` is a fourth source: the deriver reads the `command` of every mode and writes the `MODE_COMMANDS` table the advisor uses to attach a slash command to a hub recommendation.\n21: \n22: ---\n23: \n24: ## 2. CONTENTS\n25: \n26: | File | Responsibility | Authority |\n27: |---|---|---|\n28: | `command-bridges.generated.json` | Stores the derived command-bridge projection. | Generated, do not edit |\n29: | `derive-command-bridges.cjs` | Reads live metadata and authored inputs, validates uniqueness and writes the projection. | Authored deriver |\n30: | `scoring-compatibility.json` | Holds TypeScript and Python scoring compatibility mappings. | Authored input |\n31: | `shadow-diff.md` | Records comparison evidence between generated and live bridge authorities. | Authored evidence |\n32: \n33: ---\n34: \n35: ## 3. DERIVATION FLOW\n36: \n37: ```text\n38: skill command metadata files\n39: scoring-compatibility.json\n40: .skilled/commands/doctor/_routes.yaml\n41: hub mode-registry.json files\n42:                          |\n43:                          v\n44:               derive-command-bridges.cjs\n45:                          |\n46:                          v\n47:               command-bridges.generated.json\n48: ```\n49: \n50: The deriver also updates generated projection blocks in the live TypeScript and Python scoring sources. Its `GENERATED COMMAND ROWS` block in `lib/command-attach.ts` holds `COMMAND_ROWS`, one row per id a scorer can return for an enabled entry, covering the entry id and every enabled runtime id. Each row carries the runnable slash command, its derived policy and the owning skill. The same block holds `COMMAND_POLICIES`, one policy per runnable string, so a surface that attaches a command can name its policy without a second lookup. It writes the `MODE_COMMANDS` block into the same file, one row per hub mode that names a command, and fails when a mode names a command with no file under `.skilled/commands/`. Each TypeScript bridge in `lib/scorer/projection.ts` carries an `ownerSkill`, the skill that owns its command. The generated JSON and generated source blocks are outputs, not hand-edited inputs.\n51: \n52: Each bridge row with TypeScript enabled also carries an `embeddingText`, the plain sentence a scoring vector is built from. A Python-only row carries none. A metadata row leads with its `userIntent.job` sentence and adds every `userIntent.ownedSignals` phrase, because both are written in the asker's voice. The entry `description` stands in for the job only when the record states no job. A doctor row builds it from the router frontmatter `description` and the `trigger_phrases` of the route. The deriver drops each slash command string before the parts are joined, because a name left in would hand the scorer a second copy of the token it already matches literally. The field is derived on every run and never authored, so edit the sources rather than the JSON.\n53: \n54: ---\n55: \n56: ## 4. DOCTOR BRIDGES\n57: \n58: Ids are `command-doctor-<command>-<target>` when the doctor command owns more than one route, and `command-doctor-<command>` otherwise. `skillId` and `ownerMode` come from the router's `skill_agent` tag. A router without the tag fails the deriver with a named error.\n59: \n60: The runnable string is the command plus its target when the command owns more than one route. A standalone entry may set `runnable`, as `/doctor:env` does with `/doctor:env list`.\n61: \n62: A new route needs a hand-written entry in both the `typescript` and `python` lists in `scoring-compatibility.json`. Each entry carries only `inventoryId`, `order`, `id` (equal to `inventoryId`) and an empty `description`.\n63: \n64: The deriver fills the TypeScript name, keywords, intent signals and domains from the manifest, and the Python slash marker from the runnable string. A route marked `default_target` in `_routes.yaml` also gets the bare command (for example `/doctor:ci`) as a TypeScript keyword and a Python slash marker, so the bare typed command reaches it. A typed `<command> <target>` names that target, so the bare command inside it does not also match the default target. Python matches a doctor bridge only on a typed slash command.\n65: \n66: A doctor bridge passes on its own wording (an explicit-author phrase match). When none passes and no `/doctor:` command was typed, the scorer asks `/doctor:helper`'s own ranker, `doctor-helper-match.cjs`, whether the prompt describes a doctor problem. If it does, `command-doctor-helper` passes, and `/doctor:helper` then names the command. With no skill passing, a ranker score of 10 or more is enough, or 6 or more when the prompt names a problem. Beside a passing skill, the prompt must name a wrong state or a health check, such as `is not registered`, `never converges` or `turn off`. A `/doctor:` command counts as typed when the prompt starts with `/doctor:` or when it directly follows `run`, `execute`, `start`, `launch`, `use` or `kick off` outside quotes and backticks. A quoted command or a bare mid-sentence mention does not count. A typed command puts its own passing bridge first. A prompt that contains one of a bridge's `lead_phrases` also puts that passing bridge first, so `check my environment variables` leads with `/doctor:env`. For other prompts, a passing skill keeps first place and passing doctor bridges sit right after the second passing skill or the only passing skill. This keeps a doctor line within the CLI's three shown results. A typed command shows its matching runnable string on the top line. A skill-first brief shows `Also: /doctor:<command> <target>` on the second line. The Python fallback matches a doctor bridge only on a typed slash command.\n67: \n68: Each `mcp_subroutes` entry becomes the bridge `command-doctor-mcp-<sub_action>`, with the runnable string `/doctor:mcp <sub_action>` and its owner from the `skill_agent` tag in `mcp.md`. Every derived doctor bridge is active in both scorers, and `doctor-phrase-reach.cjs` proves each one.\n69: \n70: Check every admitted phrase with `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/doctor-phrase-reach.cjs`.\n71: \n72: Every command bridge, not only a doctor bridge, passes on its own wording. `requireCommandPhraseAnchor` in `lib/scorer/fusion.ts` drops a passing command row below threshold unless the explicit-author lane matched one of its phrases or its slash string. The one other way in is meaning: a real embedder cosine of at least 0.72 against the row's `embeddingText` vector, with a 0.02 lead over the next command row. That needs the local embedder, so without it every command row passes on its wording alone. A prompt that asks for no change rules out every command on meaning, and a read-only question admits only a bridge marked `readOnly`.\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: ## 5. STANDALONE SKILL COMMANDS\n77: \n78: A standalone skill has a `command-metadata.json` and no `mode-registry.json`, as `sk-prompt` and `system-spec-kit` do. Its commands are active in both scorers by construction and need no row in `scoring-compatibility.json`. The deriver builds each row from the metadata entry:\n79: \n80: - TypeScript: the `userIntent.ownedSignals` phrases become the keywords and intent signals, the command's namespace and `command` become the domains, and a `read-only` `mutating` class sets `readOnly: true`.\n81: - Python: the slash markers are the command with and without its leading slash, and `owningSkill` is the skill.\n82: \n83: A command needs at least two `userIntent.ownedSignals`, or the deriver fails. A hand row in `scoring-compatibility.json` still wins where one exists. Generated rows take orders after the highest hand-written order.\n84: \n85: ---\n86: \n87: ## 6. HUMAN EDIT BOUNDARY\n88: \n89: Humans may edit `derive-command-bridges.cjs`, `scoring-compatibility.json` and `shadow-diff.md`. Regenerate `command-bridges.generated.json` after changing an authored input or the deriver. Do not edit the generated JSON directly.\n90: \n91: ---\n92: \n93: ## 7. VALIDATION\n94: \n95: Run the deriver in check mode from the repository root:\n96: \n97: ```bash\n98: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/derive-command-bridges.cjs --check\n99: ```\n100: \n101: ---\n102: \n103: ## 8. RISK KEYS AND COMMAND POLICY\n104: \n105: The command tree `.skilled/commands/<namespace>/<name>.md` owns which commands exist. The deriver fails when a command file has no `command-metadata.json` entry and no doctor route. It fails again when a declaration names a command with no file.\n106: \n107: A `command-metadata.json` entry and a doctor route or action carry the same risk keys. A value outside the accepted set fails the deriver before any policy is derived.\n108: \n109: | Risk key | Accepted values | Applies to |\n110: |---|---|---|\n111: | `mutating` | `read-only`, `add-only`, `mutates` | Every metadata entry and every doctor route or action |\n112: | `gate3_location` | A non-empty string naming what the command writes | Required on every metadata entry |\n113: | `ask_first` | `settings`, `outside-repo`, `spend`, `relaunch` | Optional on both records |\n114: | `write_scope` | `spec-folder` | Optional on both records |\n115: \n116: `derivePolicy` reads those keys in order and stops at the first match.\n117: \n118: | Step | Condition | Policy |\n119: |---|---|---|\n120: | 1 | `ask_first` lists at least one reason | `ask` |\n121: | 2 | A doctor route or action classed `mutates` | `ask` |\n122: | 3 | `mutating` is `read-only` | `run` |\n123: | 4 | `mutating` is `add-only` with `write_scope` set to `spec-folder` | `run` |\n124: | 5 | Any other record | `start` |\n125: \n126: `commandPolicies` in `command-bridges.generated.json` holds one record per command, or one record per route or action for a doctor command. Each record carries five keys: `command`, `route`, `mutating`, `askFirst`, `policy`. Nothing scores on it yet.\n127: \n128: ---\n129: \n130: ## 9. RELATED\n131: \n132: - [`Skill-advisor scripts`](../README.md)"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794540803378,"end":1794540804134}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794540805822,"sessionID":"ses_e2d08c914ffeKxv7Qy4XSnG7U3","part":{"id":"prt_1d2f79ebe001wptBd7E2UZ1VY7","sessionID":"ses_e2d08c914ffeKxv7Qy4XSnG7U3","messageID":"msg_1d2f78169001szcPRYewpeFWO8","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"9deb4f24e95d42c7cdd2e65ebe072f410b3cf21c","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":8660,"output":137,"reasoning":213,"cache":{"read":12288,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794540807677,"sessionID":"ses_e2d08c914ffeKxv7Qy4XSnG7U3","part":{"id":"prt_1d2f7a5fd001g2yA4Xu62mkudy","sessionID":"ses_e2d08c914ffeKxv7Qy4XSnG7U3","messageID":"msg_1d2f79ec3001xH62ziPOkMiMXj","type":"step-start","snapshot":"ce3288522de59df23a47ceba1c9acc4a219a5e55"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794540809615,"sessionID":"ses_e2d08c914ffeKxv7Qy4XSnG7U3","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2f7ace60011EOQAHZ2wr1Dwc","sessionID":"ses_e2d08c914ffeKxv7Qy4XSnG7U3","messageID":"msg_1d2f79ec3001xH62ziPOkMiMXj","type":"tool","id":"call_b2d8da03cb734cfd9f9ca0ae","tool":"skill","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"id":"sk-doc"},"output":"<skill_content name=\"sk-doc\">\n# Skill: sk-doc\n\n<!-- Keywords: sk-doc, documentation, markdown, authoring, parent-hub, mode-registry, hub-router, workflowmode, packetkind, sk-create-skill, sk-create-readme, sk-create-agent, sk-create-command, sk-create-feature-catalog, sk-create-manual-testing-playbook, sk-create-benchmark, sk-create-changelog, sk-create-diff, sk-create-repo-rule, sk-create-frontmatter, sk-create-goal, sk-create-with-human-voice, sk-create-quality-control, human-voice-rules, hvr, shared-backbone, create-quality-control-pipeline -->\n\n# Documentation Authoring Hub (sk-doc)\n\nOne advisor identity, fifteen workflow modes across fourteen packets, one shared sk-create-quality-control backbone.\n`sk-doc` is the parent hub for documentation and OpenCode-component authoring.\nIt holds NO per-packet logic: it routes by `workflowMode` through `mode-registry.json`, and each packet keeps its own contract in its nested folder.\nThe cross-cutting sk-create-quality-control pipeline (validators, global standards, frontmatter/llms/template assets) lives once in `shared/` and is consumed by every packet.\n\n**Version authority.** This file's `version` frontmatter is the hub's release version and matches the newest entry under `changelog/`; `description.json`, `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json` carry the same value, so every hub-root JSON routing file states the same release; `ROUTER.md` keeps its own document version.\n\n---\n\n## 1. WHEN TO USE\n\nUse this skill for documentation and OpenCode-component authoring, and for document-quality work. The hub classifies the request, resolves a `workflowMode`, and loads the matching nested packet.\n\n| Mode | Use it for | Packet | Command |\n|------|------------|--------|---------|\n| **sk-create-skill** | Scaffold an OpenCode skill (and, via `sk-create-skill-parent`, a parent hub with nested mode packets) | `sk-create-skill/` | `/create:skill`, `/create:skill-parent` |\n| **sk-create-readme** | Author a folder README, general or code-folder | `sk-create-readme/` | `/create:readme` |\n| **sk-create-agent** | Scaffold an OpenCode agent (permission/authority frontmatter) | `sk-create-agent/` | `/create:agent` |\n| **sk-create-command** | Scaffold an OpenCode slash command (argument-hint + allowed-tools + router/presentation split) | `sk-create-command/` | `/create:command` |\n| **sk-create-feature-catalog** | Author a feature-catalog inventory package | `sk-create-feature-catalog/` | `/create:feature-catalog` |\n| **sk-create-manual-testing-playbook** | Author a manual-testing-playbook package | `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/` | `/create:manual-testing-playbook` |\n| **sk-create-benchmark** | Author MCP-promotion and behavior packages or inputs | `sk-create-benchmark/` | `/create:benchmark` |\n| **sk-create-changelog** | Author a global component changelog entry (version bump + topology-aware placement). Packet-local changelogs are deprecated | `sk-create-changelog/` | `/create:changelog` |\n| **sk-create-diff** | Produce a local, Git-free before/after review of an edited document (text/Markdown/HTML/DOCX/text-PDF) as a self-contained HTML report | `sk-create-diff/` | `/create:diff` |\n| **sk-create-repo-rule** | Create, revise or retire a repo-local rule under `repo-rules/` and wire it into `REPO RULES.md` (four decision tests refuse most requests) | `sk-create-repo-rule/` | `/create:repo-rule` |\n| **sk-create-with-human-voice** | Apply the Human Voice Rules to prose, or score prose against them, with a scope gate and a re-scan | `sk-create-with-human-voice/` | `/create:with-human-voice` |\n| **sk-create-frontmatter** | Own the YAML frontmatter contract: per-class field rules, the description budget, and the 4-part `version` derivation | `sk-create-frontmatter/` | — (routes via aliases) |\n| **sk-create-goal** | Author or revise a packet `goal.md`: the durable directive, three to seven self-contained completion criteria, and the phase-parent binding table (session-goal setting stays with the goal hooks) | `sk-create-goal/` | `/create:goal` |\n| **sk-create-quality-control** | Validate / score / optimize an EXISTING document (extract → DQI → HVR → validate) | `sk-create-quality-control/` | (routes via aliases) |\n\n### When NOT to Use\n- Code implementation, tests, or debugging — use `sk-code`.\n- Git worktree, branch, commit, PR, or release work — use `sk-git`.\n- Spec-folder workflow / memory / save context — use `system-spec-kit`.\n- A measured Style Reference (design tokens extracted from a live source) — use `sk-design-md-generator`.\n- The `shared/` backbone is consumed by the packets, not invoked as a user workflow.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SMART ROUTING\n\nRouting is **registry-driven at runtime and authored in `hub-router.json`** in two stages.\nStage 1 (hub → mode) selects a mode: `hub-router.json` `vocabularyClasses` is the one authored routing vocabulary.\nThe `mode-registry.json` aliases, each packet's `Keyword triggers:` line and the root `ROUTER.md` intents are checked against it.\n`sk-create-skill/scripts/ci-hub-vocabulary-source.cjs` fails on the `alias`, `stage-two`, `triggers` and `packets` legs; its `stage-two-reverse` leg only reports.\nStage 2 (mode → leaves) lives in the root [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md), which maps the request's authoring intent to the packet-local leaf resources that mode loads.\nThe two layers stay separate: the hub never emits leaf paths, and the surface router never re-decides the mode.\nThe advisor routes every documentation and authoring query to the single identity `sk-doc`.\nResolve the mode with `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-doc --prompt \"<task>\"`.\nAct on the decision: `route` loads its `targets`, `clarify` and `defer` ask, `reject` refuses.\nCompiled routing is the default.\nAn unclear documentation intent asks for disambiguation, because `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`.\nRun the compiled-routing directive in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting hub resources.\n\n---\n\n## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n\n### Terms\n\n- **`workflowMode`**: the public packet key a request resolves to, such as `sk-create-skill`.\n- **`packetKind`**: `workflow` for every sk-doc packet, because this hub has no surface axis.\n- **`backendKind`**: `template-scaffold` for the create-* generators and `sk-create-quality-control` for the quality-control mode.\n- **packet**: a nested `sk-create-*` folder that keeps its own authoring contract, references, assets and scripts.\n- **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each packet's discriminator, aliases, command and `advisorRouting`.\n- **`hub-router.json`**: stage one's signals, vocabulary classes, default fallback resource and bundle rules.\n- **`vocabularyClasses`**: the one authored routing vocabulary that the registry aliases, the `Keyword triggers:` lines and the `ROUTER.md` intents are checked against.\n- **`ROUTER.md`**: stage two's `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP`, which map an authoring intent to the packet-local leaf resources its mode loads.\n- **leaf**: a canonical `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair from `leaf-manifest.json` that a `RESOURCE_MAP` path converts to.\n- **`defer`**: the outcome that asks for disambiguation, which this hub reaches because `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`.\n- **shared backbone**: the `shared/` copy of the sk-create-quality-control pipeline that every packet consumes.\n- **`graph-metadata.json`**: the one advisor identity for the whole skill, which no packet or `shared/` directory carries.\n\n### Layout\n```\nsk-doc/\n  SKILL.md               # this routing hub (no per-packet logic)\n  ROUTER.md              # stage-two surface router (authoring intent -> leaf sets)\n  mode-registry.json     # the fifteen-mode discriminator + advisorRouting (single source of truth)\n  hub-router.json        # router signals + vocabulary classes\n  description.json       # hub advisor descriptor\n  graph-metadata.json    # the ONE advisor identity for the whole skill\n  changelog/  manual-testing-playbook/  benchmark/  feature-catalog/\n  sk-create-skill/  sk-create-readme/  sk-create-agent/  sk-create-command/\n  sk-create-feature-catalog/  sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/\n  sk-create-benchmark/  sk-create-changelog/  sk-create-diff/\n  sk-create-repo-rule/  sk-create-frontmatter/  sk-create-goal/\n  sk-create-with-human-voice/  sk-create-quality-control/    # nested workflow packets\n  scripts/               # facade symlinks -> shared/ + owning packets (tool paths only)\n  shared/                # sk-create-quality-control backbone: validators, global standards, shared assets\n```\n\nEach packet is self-contained (its own `SKILL.md`, `README.md`, `changelog/`, and moved `references/`/`assets/`/`scripts/`) and carries **no** `graph-metadata.json`, so the advisor discovers exactly one `sk-doc` identity.\n\n### Shared backbone\n`shared/` holds the universal sk-create-quality-control pipeline consumed by every packet:\n- Generic validator scripts in `shared/scripts/`.\n- Cross-cutting standards and vocabulary in `shared/references/`.\n- Shared templates in `shared/assets/`.\n\nThe generic validator `shared/scripts/cite-drift-scan.mjs` counts and resolves the `<path>.<ext>:<line>` citations in every tracked skill doc.\nIt makes zero model calls by default.\n`--jev` runs the backend and needs `--out <dir>`, so every call is recorded, and the Jev arm and the advisory live in the sibling `shared/scripts/classifier-cite-drift-scan.mjs`.\n`validate_document.py` runs the same check as a non-blocking advisory on the document it validates.\nIt prints `cite-drift advisory:` lines only when a Jev credential is stored, never changes the exit code, and `JEV_FEATURE_CITE_DRIFT=0` (older name: `SKDOC_CITE_DRIFT_CHECK=0`) skips it.\nThe `sk-doc/scripts/` root directory keeps facade symlinks pointing inward to `shared/` and the owning packets so tool paths resolve.\nThere are no hub-root `assets/` or `references/` aggregation directories: consumers reference each packet's own `assets/`/`references/` or the `shared/` backbone directly.\n\n---\n\n## 4. RULES\n\n### ✅ ALWAYS\n- **ALWAYS** resolve a packet through `mode-registry.json`; never hardcode a router mapping in the hub.\n- **ALWAYS** keep authoring contracts in the packets; the hub stays routing-only.\n- **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one identity.\n- **ALWAYS** keep the root `ROUTER.md` `RESOURCE_MAP` in sync with `leaf-manifest.json` and `leaf-aliases.json` — the leaf sets dual-read to canonical typed pairs at the one contract boundary.\n- **ALWAYS** keep the sk-create-quality-control pipeline as one shared source under `shared/`, consumed by the packets.\n- **ALWAYS** keep changelogs as real files at the hub and in each packet — never symlinked.\n\n### ⛔ NEVER\n- **NEVER** add a `graph-metadata.json` inside a packet or `shared/`.\n- **NEVER** put per-packet authoring logic in the hub.\n- **NEVER** add a surface axis or a `surfaceBundle` outcome — sk-doc is workflow-only.\n\n### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n- A new documentation workflow is needed — extend `mode-registry.json` and open a packet; do not bolt logic onto the hub.\n- A packet would become a near-empty shell (generic doctrine, no type-specific behavior) — fold it into `shared/` instead.\n\n---\n\n## 5. REFERENCES\n\n- Registry: `mode-registry.json` (fifteen modes across fourteen packets, `packetKind: workflow`).\n- Hub router: `hub-router.json` (signals + vocabulary classes).\n- Surface router: `ROUTER.md` (authoring intent to packet-local leaf sets).\n- Advisor descriptor: `description.json`; skill-graph identity: `graph-metadata.json`.\n- Packets: `sk-create-skill/`, `sk-create-readme/`, `sk-create-agent/`, `sk-create-command/`, `sk-create-feature-catalog/`, `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/`, `sk-create-benchmark/`, `sk-create-changelog/`, `sk-create-diff/`, `sk-create-repo-rule/`, `sk-create-frontmatter/`, `sk-create-goal/`, `sk-create-with-human-voice/`, `sk-create-quality-control/`.\n- Shared backbone: `shared/scripts/`, `shared/references/`, `shared/assets/`.\n- Parent-skill pattern: `sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md`.\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/ROUTER.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/failed-runs.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/results.csv</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/source.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal/results.csv</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal/source.md</file>\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>","title":"skill","metadata":{"metadata":{"name":"sk-doc","directory":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc","truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"<skill_content name=\"sk-doc\">\n# Skill: sk-doc\n\n<!-- Keywords: sk-doc, documentation, markdown, authoring, parent-hub, mode-registry, hub-router, workflowmode, packetkind, sk-create-skill, sk-create-readme, sk-create-agent, sk-create-command, sk-create-feature-catalog, sk-create-manual-testing-playbook, sk-create-benchmark, sk-create-changelog, sk-create-diff, sk-create-repo-rule, sk-create-frontmatter, sk-create-goal, sk-create-with-human-voice, sk-create-quality-control, human-voice-rules, hvr, shared-backbone, create-quality-control-pipeline -->\n\n# Documentation Authoring Hub (sk-doc)\n\nOne advisor identity, fifteen workflow modes across fourteen packets, one shared sk-create-quality-control backbone.\n`sk-doc` is the parent hub for documentation and OpenCode-component authoring.\nIt holds NO per-packet logic: it routes by `workflowMode` through `mode-registry.json`, and each packet keeps its own contract in its nested folder.\nThe cross-cutting sk-create-quality-control pipeline (validators, global standards, frontmatter/llms/template assets) lives once in `shared/` and is consumed by every packet.\n\n**Version authority.** This file's `version` frontmatter is the hub's release version and matches the newest entry under `changelog/`; `description.json`, `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json` carry the same value, so every hub-root JSON routing file states the same release; `ROUTER.md` keeps its own document version.\n\n---\n\n## 1. WHEN TO USE\n\nUse this skill for documentation and OpenCode-component authoring, and for document-quality work. The hub classifies the request, resolves a `workflowMode`, and loads the matching nested packet.\n\n| Mode | Use it for | Packet | Command |\n|------|------------|--------|---------|\n| **sk-create-skill** | Scaffold an OpenCode skill (and, via `sk-create-skill-parent`, a parent hub with nested mode packets) | `sk-create-skill/` | `/create:skill`, `/create:skill-parent` |\n| **sk-create-readme** | Author a folder README, general or code-folder | `sk-create-readme/` | `/create:readme` |\n| **sk-create-agent** | Scaffold an OpenCode agent (permission/authority frontmatter) | `sk-create-agent/` | `/create:agent` |\n| **sk-create-command** | Scaffold an OpenCode slash command (argument-hint + allowed-tools + router/presentation split) | `sk-create-command/` | `/create:command` |\n| **sk-create-feature-catalog** | Author a feature-catalog inventory package | `sk-create-feature-catalog/` | `/create:feature-catalog` |\n| **sk-create-manual-testing-playbook** | Author a manual-testing-playbook package | `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/` | `/create:manual-testing-playbook` |\n| **sk-create-benchmark** | Author MCP-promotion and behavior packages or inputs | `sk-create-benchmark/` | `/create:benchmark` |\n| **sk-create-changelog** | Author a global component changelog entry (version bump + topology-aware placement). Packet-local changelogs are deprecated | `sk-create-changelog/` | `/create:changelog` |\n| **sk-create-diff** | Produce a local, Git-free before/after review of an edited document (text/Markdown/HTML/DOCX/text-PDF) as a self-contained HTML report | `sk-create-diff/` | `/create:diff` |\n| **sk-create-repo-rule** | Create, revise or retire a repo-local rule under `repo-rules/` and wire it into `REPO RULES.md` (four decision tests refuse most requests) | `sk-create-repo-rule/` | `/create:repo-rule` |\n| **sk-create-with-human-voice** | Apply the Human Voice Rules to prose, or score prose against them, with a scope gate and a re-scan | `sk-create-with-human-voice/` | `/create:with-human-voice` |\n| **sk-create-frontmatter** | Own the YAML frontmatter contract: per-class field rules, the description budget, and the 4-part `version` derivation | `sk-create-frontmatter/` | — (routes via aliases) |\n| **sk-create-goal** | Author or revise a packet `goal.md`: the durable directive, three to seven self-contained completion criteria, and the phase-parent binding table (session-goal setting stays with the goal hooks) | `sk-create-goal/` | `/create:goal` |\n| **sk-create-quality-control** | Validate / score / optimize an EXISTING document (extract → DQI → HVR → validate) | `sk-create-quality-control/` | (routes via aliases) |\n\n### When NOT to Use\n- Code implementation, tests, or debugging — use `sk-code`.\n- Git worktree, branch, commit, PR, or release work — use `sk-git`.\n- Spec-folder workflow / memory / save context — use `system-spec-kit`.\n- A measured Style Reference (design tokens extracted from a live source) — use `sk-design-md-generator`.\n- The `shared/` backbone is consumed by the packets, not invoked as a user workflow.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SMART ROUTING\n\nRouting is **registry-driven at runtime and authored in `hub-router.json`** in two stages.\nStage 1 (hub → mode) selects a mode: `hub-router.json` `vocabularyClasses` is the one authored routing vocabulary.\nThe `mode-registry.json` aliases, each packet's `Keyword triggers:` line and the root `ROUTER.md` intents are checked against it.\n`sk-create-skill/scripts/ci-hub-vocabulary-source.cjs` fails on the `alias`, `stage-two`, `triggers` and `packets` legs; its `stage-two-reverse` leg only reports.\nStage 2 (mode → leaves) lives in the root [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md), which maps the request's authoring intent to the packet-local leaf resources that mode loads.\nThe two layers stay separate: the hub never emits leaf paths, and the surface router never re-decides the mode.\nThe advisor routes every documentation and authoring query to the single identity `sk-doc`.\nResolve the mode with `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-doc --prompt \"<task>\"`.\nAct on the decision: `route` loads its `targets`, `clarify` and `defer` ask, `reject` refuses.\nCompiled routing is the default.\nAn unclear documentation intent asks for disambiguation, because `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`.\nRun the compiled-routing directive in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting hub resources.\n\n---\n\n## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n\n### Terms\n\n- **`workflowMode`**: the public packet key a request resolves to, such as `sk-create-skill`.\n- **`packetKind`**: `workflow` for every sk-doc packet, because this hub has no surface axis.\n- **`backendKind`**: `template-scaffold` for the create-* generators and `sk-create-quality-control` for the quality-control mode.\n- **packet**: a nested `sk-create-*` folder that keeps its own authoring contract, references, assets and scripts.\n- **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each packet's discriminator, aliases, command and `advisorRouting`.\n- **`hub-router.json`**: stage one's signals, vocabulary classes, default fallback resource and bundle rules.\n- **`vocabularyClasses`**: the one authored routing vocabulary that the registry aliases, the `Keyword triggers:` lines and the `ROUTER.md` intents are checked against.\n- **`ROUTER.md`**: stage two's `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP`, which map an authoring intent to the packet-local leaf resources its mode loads.\n- **leaf**: a canonical `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair from `leaf-manifest.json` that a `RESOURCE_MAP` path converts to.\n- **`defer`**: the outcome that asks for disambiguation, which this hub reaches because `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`.\n- **shared backbone**: the `shared/` copy of the sk-create-quality-control pipeline that every packet consumes.\n- **`graph-metadata.json`**: the one advisor identity for the whole skill, which no packet or `shared/` directory carries.\n\n### Layout\n```\nsk-doc/\n  SKILL.md               # this routing hub (no per-packet logic)\n  ROUTER.md              # stage-two surface router (authoring intent -> leaf sets)\n  mode-registry.json     # the fifteen-mode discriminator + advisorRouting (single source of truth)\n  hub-router.json        # router signals + vocabulary classes\n  description.json       # hub advisor descriptor\n  graph-metadata.json    # the ONE advisor identity for the whole skill\n  changelog/  manual-testing-playbook/  benchmark/  feature-catalog/\n  sk-create-skill/  sk-create-readme/  sk-create-agent/  sk-create-command/\n  sk-create-feature-catalog/  sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/\n  sk-create-benchmark/  sk-create-changelog/  sk-create-diff/\n  sk-create-repo-rule/  sk-create-frontmatter/  sk-create-goal/\n  sk-create-with-human-voice/  sk-create-quality-control/    # nested workflow packets\n  scripts/               # facade symlinks -> shared/ + owning packets (tool paths only)\n  shared/                # sk-create-quality-control backbone: validators, global standards, shared assets\n```\n\nEach packet is self-contained (its own `SKILL.md`, `README.md`, `changelog/`, and moved `references/`/`assets/`/`scripts/`) and carries **no** `graph-metadata.json`, so the advisor discovers exactly one `sk-doc` identity.\n\n### Shared backbone\n`shared/` holds the universal sk-create-quality-control pipeline consumed by every packet:\n- Generic validator scripts in `shared/scripts/`.\n- Cross-cutting standards and vocabulary in `shared/references/`.\n- Shared templates in `shared/assets/`.\n\nThe generic validator `shared/scripts/cite-drift-scan.mjs` counts and resolves the `<path>.<ext>:<line>` citations in every tracked skill doc.\nIt makes zero model calls by default.\n`--jev` runs the backend and needs `--out <dir>`, so every call is recorded, and the Jev arm and the advisory live in the sibling `shared/scripts/classifier-cite-drift-scan.mjs`.\n`validate_document.py` runs the same check as a non-blocking advisory on the document it validates.\nIt prints `cite-drift advisory:` lines only when a Jev credential is stored, never changes the exit code, and `JEV_FEATURE_CITE_DRIFT=0` (older name: `SKDOC_CITE_DRIFT_CHECK=0`) skips it.\nThe `sk-doc/scripts/` root directory keeps facade symlinks pointing inward to `shared/` and the owning packets so tool paths resolve.\nThere are no hub-root `assets/` or `references/` aggregation directories: consumers reference each packet's own `assets/`/`references/` or the `shared/` backbone directly.\n\n---\n\n## 4. RULES\n\n### ✅ ALWAYS\n- **ALWAYS** resolve a packet through `mode-registry.json`; never hardcode a router mapping in the hub.\n- **ALWAYS** keep authoring contracts in the packets; the hub stays routing-only.\n- **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one identity.\n- **ALWAYS** keep the root `ROUTER.md` `RESOURCE_MAP` in sync with `leaf-manifest.json` and `leaf-aliases.json` — the leaf sets dual-read to canonical typed pairs at the one contract boundary.\n- **ALWAYS** keep the sk-create-quality-control pipeline as one shared source under `shared/`, consumed by the packets.\n- **ALWAYS** keep changelogs as real files at the hub and in each packet — never symlinked.\n\n### ⛔ NEVER\n- **NEVER** add a `graph-metadata.json` inside a packet or `shared/`.\n- **NEVER** put per-packet authoring logic in the hub.\n- **NEVER** add a surface axis or a `surfaceBundle` outcome — sk-doc is workflow-only.\n\n### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n- A new documentation workflow is needed — extend `mode-registry.json` and open a packet; do not bolt logic onto the hub.\n- A packet would become a near-empty shell (generic doctrine, no type-specific behavior) — fold it into `shared/` instead.\n\n---\n\n## 5. REFERENCES\n\n- Registry: `mode-registry.json` (fifteen modes across fourteen packets, `packetKind: workflow`).\n- Hub router: `hub-router.json` (signals + vocabulary classes).\n- Surface router: `ROUTER.md` (authoring intent to packet-local leaf sets).\n- Advisor descriptor: `description.json`; skill-graph identity: `graph-metadata.json`.\n- Packets: `sk-create-skill/`, `sk-create-readme/`, `sk-create-agent/`, `sk-create-command/`, `sk-create-feature-catalog/`, `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/`, `sk-create-benchmark/`, `sk-create-changelog/`, `sk-create-diff/`, `sk-create-repo-rule/`, `sk-create-frontmatter/`, `sk-create-goal/`, `sk-create-with-human-voice/`, `sk-create-quality-control/`.\n- Shared backbone: `shared/scripts/`, `shared/references/`, `shared/assets/`.\n- Parent-skill pattern: `sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md`.\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/ROUTER.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/failed-runs.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/results.csv</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/source.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal/results.csv</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal/source.md</file>\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"id\":\"sk-doc\"}"},"time":{"start":1794540809446,"end":1794540809615}}}}
